    "case_summary": "A 34-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1 (previous cesarean section), presented at 4 weeks 5 days of amenorrhea with a 1-day history of epigastric pain radiating to the lower abdomen. She denied fever, nausea, vomiting, or vaginal bleeding. On examination, she was afebrile, hemodynamically stable, and had epigastric tenderness without rebound or guarding; Murphy’s and McBurney’s signs were negative. Laboratory testing showed a white blood cell count of 13.54×10^9/L and a β-hCG level of 523 IU/L; C-reactive protein was not performed. Transvaginal ultrasound revealed an empty uterus with normal endometrial thickness, a 1.2 cm left adnexal structure with minimal vascularity posterior to the left ovary, and a small amount of free fluid in the pouch of Douglas. Given the short duration of amenorrhea, absence of vaginal bleeding, β-hCG level below the discriminatory zone, and an empty uterine cavity on ultrasound, a diagnostic laparoscopy was offered to evaluate for a possible ectopic pregnancy.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Acute appendicitis\nB. given the imaging and lab findings\nC. Ectopic pregnancy was initially suspected given the imaging and lab findings\nD. Intraoperative inspection during laparoscopy excluded ectopic pregnancy\nE. Acute appendicitis was diagnosed when pus was noted in the pouch of Douglas originating from the inflamed appendix\nF. Histologic examination confirmed the diagnosis\nG. None\nH. None\n",
